## 3. Google Docs as a prose source

**Native Markdown export shipped 2024-07-16 (Rapid) / 2024-07-31 (Scheduled)** to *all* account types — Workspace, Workspace Individual, and personal Google accounts.

Endpoints:
```
GET https://docs.google.com/document/d/{DOC_ID}/export?format=markdown   # link-shared docs
GET https://docs.google.com/document/d/{DOC_ID}/export?format=html
GET https://www.googleapis.com/drive/v3/files/{DOC_ID}/export?mimeType=text%2Fmarkdown
```
Supported `files.export` MIME types for Docs: `text/markdown`, `text/html`, `application/zip` (Web Page HTML, zipped), `text/plain`, `application/pdf`, `application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document`, `application/vnd.oasis.opendocument.text`, `application/rtf`, `application/epub+zip`.

**Quality by format:**

| Format | Quality |
|---|---|
| `text/markdown` | Clean. Headings, lists, links, bold/italic, tables all map. **Images become base64 `data:` URIs that render broken.** |
| `text/html` | Messy: inline `style` on nearly every node, generated `c1`/`c3`/`c8` classes, empty `<span>` wrappers, `<b>` instead of `<strong>`. |
| `application/zip` | **Best for docs with images** — HTML plus real image binaries in `images/`. Still needs the same HTML sanitising. |
| Docs API `documents.get` | Full structural JSON. Maximum fidelity (incl. footnotes as `document.footnotes`), maximum work. Only worth it if footnotes matter. |

**Decision rule:** if the Doc has no images → `text/markdown`, done. If it has images → `application/zip`, sanitise the HTML, push each image through the sharp pipeline.

Sanitiser:

```ts
import { unified } from "unified";
import rehypeParse from "rehype-parse";
import rehypeStringify from "rehype-stringify";
import { visit } from "unist-util-visit";

const cleanGoogleHtml = () => (tree: any) => {
  visit(tree, "element", (node: any) => {
    delete node.properties?.style;
    delete node.properties?.id;
    if (Array.isArray(node.properties?.className))
      node.properties.className = node.properties.className
        .filter((c: string) => !/^c\d+$/.test(c));      // drop c1, c3, c8…
    if (node.tagName === "b") node.tagName = "strong";
    if (node.tagName === "i") node.tagName = "em";
    // Google wraps every external link in a redirector
    if (node.tagName === "a" && typeof node.properties?.href === "string") {
      const m = /google\.com\/url\?q=([^&]+)/.exec(node.properties.href);
      if (m) node.properties.href = decodeURIComponent(m[1]);
    }
  });
};

export const docHtmlToClean = (html: string) =>
  unified().use(rehypeParse, { fragment: true })
           .use(cleanGoogleHtml)
           .use(rehypeStringify)
           .processSync(html).toString();
```

Note the link-redirector rewrite — Google rewrites every external `href` to `https://www.google.com/url?q=…`, which will otherwise ship on the live site and leak referrers.

**Libraries:** `AnandChowdhary/docs-markdown` (converts a Docs API response to Markdown), `evbacher/gd2md-html` (the Docs→Markdown Workspace add-on; creates *placeholder* image markup you must fill in manually — which is exactly the base64 problem restated).

**Sheet cell vs Doc:** a Sheet cell holds ~50,000 characters (UNVERIFIED — widely reported, not confirmed by me) but has no headings, links, or paragraph structure, and its revision history is per-cell noise. Use a cell under ~500 chars; use a Doc above. Implement as a `body` column plus an optional `body_doc_url` column that overrides it.

