**Recommendation: Google-only content plane, Git-backed build, Cloudflare Pages hosting.**

The decisive argument is not technical. He is already fluent in Sheets and Docs. Every alternative asks a 50-something non-technical professor to learn a new tool for a task he does maybe twice a month — which is precisely the frequency at which an unfamiliar tool gets abandoned and the site goes stale. Nothing here has a technical advantage large enough to outweigh "he already knows how to use it."

**On the hybrid: architect for it, don't ship it.** Adding Sveltia for the blog on day one means a second login and a second mental model, against his stated preference. Instead, write each fetched Doc to `content/blog/{slug}.md` and **commit it**. The repo then already contains exactly the Markdown-with-frontmatter tree Sveltia or Keystatic expects. If in six months he wants live preview, or a student wants to write posts, point Sveltia at `content/blog/` and it works immediately with zero migration. Cheapest possible option on the future.

**Hosting: Cloudflare Pages, not Vercel Hobby.** Vercel's fair-use page defines commercial usage as *"any Deployment used for the purpose of financial gain of **anyone** involved in **any part of the production** of the project, including a paid employee or consultant writing the code,"* explicitly listing *"Receiving payment to create, update, or host the site."* If Divyansh is paid anything, Vercel Hobby is a policy violation and a suspension risk on a professor's public site. Cloudflare Pages Free permits commercial use: 500 builds/month, unlimited bandwidth, 20-min build timeout, 20,000 files, 25 MiB max asset, 100 custom domains. Build in GitHub Actions (unlimited free on a public repo; 2,000 min/month private) and deploy via `wrangler pages deploy`.

**Continuity plan (a first-class requirement):** commit the fetched JSON and Markdown, not just the built HTML. Write `docs/HANDOVER.md` (where the Sheet lives, where the service-account key lives and how to rotate it, how to trigger a build manually, how to run locally). Add `pnpm run eject` to dump every collection to plain Markdown + frontmatter. Pin exact versions, add Renovate. Record a 10-minute screen capture of his real publish workflow. A site whose content is Markdown in Git and whose source of truth is the professor's own spreadsheet survives any developer leaving; a bespoke undocumented pipeline does not.
